   ALBUQUERQUE, NM, NOV 09-13, 1996},
Organization = {Soc Amer Foresters},
Abstract = {Half of the world's biodiversity is found in the tropics; it is affected
   by disbalances created by rural inhabitants when they try to fulfill
   their wants and needs. In Mexico there are 56 indigenous groups still
   living across the country, they amount to six and a half million people.
   The rural land belongs mostly to 26,000 ``comunidades{''} and
   ``ejidos{''} which have deep roots in Spanish rules and the Agrarian
   Reform, respectively. Customs of each ethnic group are unique, either in
   the way they use the products gathered from nature or in their artistic
   manifestations. The so called ``maize culture{''} which identifies
   Mexico in the world, because maize is our basic food, has made in the
   course of time, that religious manifestations of native groups are
   reflected in their ancient Holy cities. The knowledge that we must not
   lose form these cultures are their soil management techniques and their
   crop varieties that are adapted to varied conditions of soil fertility.},
