   Urban Scale (SBE), Thessaloniki, GREECE, OCT 16-19, 2016},
Organization = {Aristotle Univ Thessaloniki, Fac Engn, Dept Civil Engn, Lab Building
   Construct \& Building Phys; Tech Chamber Greece},
Abstract = {Data centers are amongst the most complex and energy demanding building
   environments. The trends of increasing data center capacity and power
   use by information technology equipment, re-emphasize the need to reduce
   cooling loads, first cost for HVAC equipment, and finally related energy
   and operational costs. The first part of the paper presents an overview
   of relevant information and available resources for the efficient design
   and operation of these critical environments. The second part considers
   two large data centers located in Athens that serve two major Hellenic
   banks as case studies in order to investigate and quantify the potential
   measures for improving their energy performance. The two facilities were
   audited and actual energy consumption were used for an energy
   assessment. According to simulation results of various efficiency
   measures, the Power Usage Effectiveness may be improved from 2.49 to
   2.11 for the first case study and from 1.89 to 1.66 for the second one.
